-- title:
--   Eisenstein polynomials over W₀[[t]] are prime
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime natural number with $2 \le q$, and let $W_0$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain and a discrete valuation ring whose maximal ideal is the principal ideal generated by the image of $q$, i.e. $\mathfrak m_{W_0} = (q)$. Let $g$ be a polynomial in one variable over the power series ring $\mathrm{PowerSeries}\,W_0 = W_0[[t]]$ subject to: $g$ is monic; $g$ has degree (`natDegree`) equal to $q-1$; every coefficient $g_i$ with $i < q-1$ lies in the maximal ideal of the local ring $W_0[[t]]$; and the constant coefficient $g_0$ equals $q \cdot u$ for some unit $u$ of $W_0[[t]]$. The conclusion is twofold: $g$ is a prime element of the polynomial ring $W_0[[t]][X]$, and the quotient $\mathrm{AdjoinRoot}\,g = W_0[[t]][X]/(g)$ is an integral domain. Only the inequality $2 \le q$, not the primality of $q$, enters the proof.
--
--   This is the Eisenstein criterion in the form needed for the two-dimensional regular local base $W_0[[t]]$: a monic polynomial whose lower coefficients lie in the maximal ideal and whose constant coefficient is a uniformiser times a unit generates a prime ideal. It is used in the construction of local models for modular curves, where rings of the shape $W_0[[t]][X]/(g)$ are identified with completed local rings at supersingular points.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Polynomial_prime_and_isDomain_adjoinRoot_of_monic_of_coeff_mem_maximalIdeal_powerSeries_of_coeff_zero_eq_natCast_mul.lean

